Professor,\
\
I am beginning to think about research topics for the AI for robotics course. My foremost interest\
in AI for robotics lies in semi-embodied agent assistants, especially for assisting in home automation,\
day to day tasks, and engineering projects. I would like to pick a research topic related to this but I want to make sure it is academically robust enough to make a good topic for a research paper. I have narrowed down some topics that I could use some feedback on:\
\
1) Proactive assistance\
     -Current systems like this (alexa, google home, chatgpt) are still input-output oriented. They are very   good at problem solving and completing tasks, but you still have to ask them to do it. I believe this is a limitation because an action still requires manual input from the user. I believe a step forward is an agent that knows when to perform an action proactively.\
\
2) multi-modal reference problem solving\
	If a user says something to the system like \'93Turn that off\'94, a purely text based system most likely doesnt know what \'93that\'94 is. However, if this agent is multi-modal and has access to video, sound, state, etc, it would be able to infer what \'93that\'94 is and act accordingly. The research question would be how to fuse multi-modal contextual information for natural-language grounding in embodied assistants\
\
3) world awareness models/persistent physical memory\
	Instead of text-based episodic memory like \'93user put multimeter on the bench yesterday\'94, how might world state data be better handled? something like \'93multimeter.location = electronics_bench\'94 in a database or graph could be a better solution.\
\
\
\
Note: It seems like these are all interconnected and perhaps inseperable. Proactive assistance would require both multi-modal reference problem solving and world awareness. But what would actually \'91ping\'92 the agent into proactive assistance? We can use the analogy of human senses here. Rather than like an FPGA where a clock determines when deterministic actions are made, perhaps its better for an agent to have \'91senses\'92. But how do senses work? \
